1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,900
At 8:40, Project Meridian’s overrun is validated,
escalated, and explained.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:10,670
The agent proposes moving two shared architects
from Project Beacon to Meridian.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,500
On Meridian alone,
the proposal looks excellent.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:20,360
EAC falls from $88M to $84M,
and the forecast delay shrinks from 21 days to 8.

5
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:25,900
But Beacon now slips 18 days,
and its EAC rises by $2.5M.

6
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:30,780
A $12M acceptance milestone
also moves into the next quarter.

7
00:00:31,050 --> 00:00:36,600
Across the portfolio,
the improvement is only $1.5M.

8
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:45,050
The dashboard promised $4M. A phased-scope alternative
improves EAC by $2.5M with no Beacon impact, but leaves Meridian four days later.

9
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:50,200
Now ask which trade-off fits portfolio priority—
not which project looks better.

10
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:55,700
Simulate cost, schedule, cash timing, dependencies,
and approvals before recommending recovery.

11
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:59,510
Optimize the portfolio,
not the loudest project.
